    <string name="cadentia_about_close">Done</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_cordas_body">The Cordas mode came from Phelipi\'s Cordas app (github.com/phelipiii/cordas): the physically modelled guitar and the idea of reading your hands through the camera are his. The synthesis and the geometry were rewritten in native Kotlin, with the guitar body as a bank of resonators and hand tracking by Google\'s MediaPipe, on the device.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_cordas_credit">Original idea and implementation by Phelipi. Ported to native Kotlin with permission.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_cordas_title">Where Cordas came from</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_licenses_intro">Cadentia uses third-party open source software. Below are the copyright notice and license of each, as those licenses require.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_link_community">Community and support</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_link_licenses">Licenses</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_link_privacy">Privacy policy</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_link_site">RoqueOS website</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_link_terms">Terms of use</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_makers_title">Who makes it</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_phelipi_role">Author of Cordas. The physically modelled guitar and the idea of reading your hands through the camera are his — Cadentia\'s Cordas mode is his implementation, ported to native Kotlin with permission.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_privacy_body">Cadentia has no account of its own, asks for no sign-up, and uses no tracking or ads. The microphone is used only by the tuner, live, recording nothing. Your songs and recordings stay on the device. If you connect to RoqueOS, the app stores only that connection\'s credential, and disconnecting erases it.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_privacy_title">Privacy</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_rafael_role">Musician. Cadentia exists because he asked for it, and instrument separation was his idea. He is the one who sets the bar for what actually helps someone practising with the instrument in hand.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_roque_role">Developer of Cadentia and creator of RoqueOS. Twenty-five years in technology, currently at Bradesco, previously Mercado Livre, Netshoes and Avanade, and an MBA from UFSCar.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_roqueos_body">Cadentia was born inside RoqueOS, an operating system that runs in the browser, with dozens of apps, its own server and local AI. The music app from there became this native app. Connect the two and you open on your phone the songs already in your RoqueOS.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_roqueos_title">Where it came from</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_separation_body">Splits a song into vocals, drums, bass and everything else, so you can drop one instrument and play it yourself. The model runs on the device itself, works offline, and the music never leaves the device.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_separation_title">Instrument separation</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_tagline">A pocket studio for people who study music.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_title">About</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_what_body">Tuner, metronome, drums, piano, multitrack recorder, tablature and instrument separation, in one app. All free, with no ads.</string>
    <string name="cadentia_about_what_title">What it is</string>
